A||||51||maryjane||Want to try Linux||||1||
Z||000000||maryjane||10-28-2001||12:18 PM||||I'm getting tired of MS freezes and 'system has become unstable' stuff. I'd like to try something else besides Windows. What ver of Linux would be good to learn on? I want to install it on one of my older machines first to get aquainted with it before loading it on my 'good' computer. Need recomendations with system requirements if possible. Thanks,<BR>Don||||reg||1
Z||000001||btoth||10-28-2001||12:23 PM||||Probably the latest versions of Mandrake or Redhat would be a good starting point.  If you download it, it's free.  You can buy them from the store and they come with a small install guide.  For the price though, I'd just download the latest version, burn it on a CD and then go buy a book at a bookstore that looks easy to understand.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>[EDIT]<P>Most Linux system will even run on an old 386.  Of course, not all software likes running on an old computer.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by btoth (edited 10-28-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000002||SteveJ||10-28-2001||01:13 PM||||Linux is not ready for prime time. I must have loaded it at least five times and fromated it every time.<P>Not as smooth. Not as polished. A real pain in the ass to load drivers. I have never been able to get it to recognize my Tulip based network card. And it never sees the Sound Blaster AWE.<P>If your library has a computer section it may have books with all three cd's of Red Hat right there. The Dummies book and the Unleased book both have them. Probably red Hat 7.0.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Mach10||10-28-2001||01:19 PM||||Unless you are running a home network and need servers, or are an experienced programmer, Linux will do nothing for you. Sorry, we're ALL stuck with Winblows. I recommend Win98 FIRST edition. Seems to be the most stable  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>So here's my question. Everyone whines and complains about how much Windows sucks arse. SO WHY HASN'T ANYONE COME UP WITH A REASONABLE ALTERNATIVE?!?!? C'mon people, it's not THAT hard....||||reg||8
Z||000004||sdm1964||10-28-2001||01:31 PM||||I had the same problem, I also have children that download tons of useless stuff. I just ugraded to windows xp and it seems to have made mine more stable, no lock ups yet with the new version. Seems to be an NT and ME mix. So far on my HP it seems to have fixed alot of bugs. ||||reg||14
Z||000005||SteveJ||10-28-2001||01:59 PM||||Eh, I'm afraid of XP. Seems all the new software from all sources has more provisions for their advertizing. Little spaces here and there where they want to cluter my screen with their message.<P>Microsoft is the worst. While constantly talking about security they never do anything about cookies. With some rare exceptions cookies are just used for advertizing. After I register on a board I just turn the cookies back off.||||reg||1
Z||000006||SteveJ||10-28-2001||02:01 PM||||Microsoft has deliberatly made no attempt to stop pop up windows.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Cliff Pennock||10-28-2001||04:21 PM||||I'm running several distributions on several machines. The easiest to install was RedHat and would be my choice for your computer at home. I don't know what SteveJ's problem with Linux was, but my installation runs waaaaaaaay better and waaaaaaaay faster than Windows on the same machine (it's dual-boot). It also had no problems whatsoever recognizing my SBLive soundcard (had a AWE32 before this and had no problems with that either). In fact, I had no problems whatsoever installing anything on it. I can do everything in Linux that I can do in Windows. Even my Geforce2 GTS runs 3D accelerated games just fine (Unreal Tournament screams on Linux). Also, for some reason my internet connection is much faster than under Windows.<P>True that Linux isn't as easy as Windows yet (when it comes to installing new programs/drivers), but the latest version of RedHat comes pretty darn close. It even has a Control Panel that's very similar to Windows'.<P>Problem is, Linux requires a whole new way of working with your computer. If you are used to Windows, Linux might seem very cumbersome at first. And of course, there isn't as much software for Linux as there is for Windows. But the nice thing about Linux is, that almost <B>all</B> software can be aquired for free on the internet - legally.<P>I work a lot with both Linux and Windows. And although I use Windows more (since I have to because of my work and since I play some Windows-only games) I like Linux much better.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||SteveJ||10-28-2001||04:47 PM||||Well I guess I am interested in this thread because I keep plugging away at Linux.<P>I have never had a good install yet and having spent 10 yrs in computers supporting my department I'm not a newbie. All that customer contact has taught me that it has to work not be fussed with to work. Linux still has to be fussed with. So, I say, "Not ready for prime time."<P>1st network card was a 3COM 10/100 (but was ISA). Current card is Lynksys PCI that uses the common DEC chip which requires the tulip.o driver in Linux. Neither Mandrake, Corel nor Red Hat has been able to set up the network system. <P>This past week I was on a Linux site where they were mentioning that IRQ is still related to the slot used despite the PCI conformation. News to me. I thought PCI took care of all that. So I'm thinking of trying again this comming week with the card moved to another slot.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Cliff Pennock||10-28-2001||04:55 PM||||That's weird, SteveJ. I have two network cards in my computer and one of them is a 3Com 10/100. It was recognized immediately by RedHat.<P>There's one thing that's <B>very</B> important though in Linux. In your MoBo's BIOS, make sure "Plug and Play OS" is set to <B>"NO"</B>. When P&P OS is set to YES, all PCI cards are switched off until the OS switches them on. This sounds like a good idea but is the cause of most conflicts in Windows (and the reason why it's so darn hard to change/reserve IRQs). Also, Linux will be unable to find the card since it's not turned on. When P&P OS is set to NO, all cards will be swiched on immediately when you boot your PC.<P>Maybe that will make all the difference for you.||||reg||1
Z||000010||SteveJ||10-28-2001||05:40 PM||||A quick reboot reveals that the BIOS is set to YES on "Plug and Play OS". You may have nailed that one Cliff.<P>I'm cooking dinner so I will wait to tomorrow to try the reinstall. See Maryjane isn't the only one who does the cooking. ( baked chicken, baked potatoes, and a baked butternut squash )||||reg||1
Z||000011||Cliff Pennock||10-28-2001||06:08 PM||||Did you already wipe Linux of your system? If not, you first want to try to set that to "NO" and reboot linux. Perhaps it will immediately "see" the NIC.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Cliff Pennock||10-28-2001||06:09 PM||||PS: also, if I can remember correctly, you can download a (DOS) program from 3Com's site with which you can set the cards P&P mode. Sometimes switching the card's mode will help also.||||reg||1
Z||000013||GTFiero1||10-28-2001||06:15 PM||||obviously there must be somthing wrong with your computers and not windows program. thats all i've ever had and its vere given me a problem, 3.1, 95, 98 and ME have all been great to me<P>------------------<BR>Adam J. B.||||reg||1
Z||000014||SteveJ||10-28-2001||06:17 PM||||Yup, I have seen that prompt, "Searching for new hardware." <P>Linux is on an unused 10 GIG. I'll give it a try tomorrow. I have started the reinstall several times in "expert" to see if I can get the netcard up so I don't know if the OS is there. I would guess it is cause I always quit when it can't see the card.||||reg||1
