Z||000017||btoth||10-28-2001||06:34 PM||||When I installed Redhat 5.2 it recogonized everything on my computer minus my Voodoo2.  At that time, 3dfx was still in business and you could download the drivers, it was no big deal.  I guess if you've never used DOS before it could seem like a real pain to setup things in Linux... most things are just text files that you have to know how to edit.  But with the proper guides it's not that big of a deal.  Heck, now AOL released an official AIM for Linux... that was the only thing I didn't have when I had it installed.  I just downloaded the latest version of Mandrake.. because Mandrake is supposed to be compatable with Redhat RPMs, etc, but with some better setup tools.  I can only imagine how good Redhat is now... like I said, 5.2 was pretty darned good.  I need to assemble a better computer than my little 486 to play with though and I'm out of room for more HDs in my computer unless I want to go SCSI.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Mach10||10-28-2001||07:19 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ray b:<BR><B>help may be coming soon a new program call<BR>lindows will run windows programs in a linix based system vapor wear right now but in progress.<BR>i have just heard of it on sharky extreme's site.<BR>i sure hope it works to beat BIG BILL and his evil system.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Not likely. My prediction is that "Lindows" will be every bit as stable as the PowerPC in PC mode  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><P>What we need is for a software company to get off it's prat and DESIGN something NEW and WORTHWHILE. Linux is NEITHER of these. Very nice, stable operating system designed for networking and large-scale computing. Practically USELESS for the average home user at this point.<BR>||||reg||4
Z||000019||maryjane||10-28-2001||07:24 PM||||It's not that windows is unmanagable to me, I'm just looking for some thing a little better, or maybe just different. Seems like everybody has an 'unstable system'at some time or another, and seemingly for no reason.<BR>I've had 'em, then went back and tried to duplicate the same senario, and -no problems.-no errors. Heard so much about Linux, I just want to try it. I've built my own systems lately, and want to go another route. One thing I've noticed. A lot of people in the last couple years get a new computer; it's ready to go, no problems. Then a few months down the road,, can't do anything with it because they didn't learn anything about dos commands ar anything progs & apps are based on. As long as f-disk has been around, there are people with $2000 systems that can't format a HD or set up partitions, or even access their registry or bios settings. Don't get me wrong, I have plenty of problems with mine. But I usually have an idea where to start troubleshooting.<BR>IE gives more trouble than anything else. Thanks for all the advice and info. I'm going to go with Redhat.||||reg||1
Z||000020||Cliff Pennock||10-28-2001||08:29 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B>Linux is NEITHER of these. Very nice, stable operating system designed for networking and large-scale computing. Practically USELESS for the average home user at this point.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I must disagree here. There really isn't anything I can <B>not</B> do in Linux that I can do in Windows. Except for a few games perhaps. And like I said earlier, most programs for Linux are free. On the other hand, there are <B>lots</B> of things I can do in Linux that I can't do in Windows.<P>The biggest problem is knowing where to find all these programs. Since all programs are free, you can't simply go to Best Buy to get your software. You need to know where to find it on the internet.||||reg||1
Z||000021||Jefrysuko||10-28-2001||10:46 PM||||Since you guys are talking computer mumbo jumbo maybe you can tell me a little about the computers I have been using at school.  They are Sun Microsystems and run an OS called FVWM 95.  I believe it is all linux based but the desktop looks somewhat similar to windows.<BR>There are also some SunRay machines which are just a monitor, keyboard and mouse.  These also run FVWM 95 and have a high speed server that runs several of the machines.  Everything runs extreamly fast and I have no complaints but I get lost in all of the linux comands and such.  I would be interested in running this FVWM 95 at home if I knew more about it.  Unfortunately Windows is just to friendly of an OS for a lazy guy like me to pass up.||||reg||1
