Z||000023||btoth||10-29-2001||09:15 AM||||X-Windows is the underlying GUI for Unix-based computers.  Much like Windows 3.1 in the way that it ran ontop of DOS.  (Actually, that would be all non-NT systems!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">)  There are different "window managers" that actually provide the interface you want.  Gnome, KDE, and others.<P>Sun is the company that developed Java.||||reg||1
Z||000024||SteveJ||10-29-2001||08:23 PM||||Well I keep slugging at it but I don't get any where. Changing BIOS did not help. Netcard moved to another slot - nothing. A Red Hat install followed by a Mandrake install followed by another Red Hat install. - Zero results. <P>Each install runs just fine. They just don't see the netcard or Sound Blaster.<P>The lack of tools to crack the problem is annoying. Lack of good onscreen documentation. Lack of a search function in what help they do have.||||reg||1
Z||000025||p4n1c||10-29-2001||08:39 PM||||I run Slackware on 3 machines at home.  K6-2 500, P100 and a P133 Thinkpad 560.<P>The only thing Linux doesn't have that Windows does is the wide range of games.  That's it, nothing more, nothing less.  <P>SteveJ, Linux(well any *NIX) actually has the most versatile and robust online documentation out there.  I don't like the installs of either Red Hat or Mandrake.<P>Look in /proc/pci and see what your netcard is showing as(the chipset).  Then recompile the kernel with that support either in the kernel or as a module.  Also compile support in for your Sound Blaster.<P>Should look like this for your ethernet card:<BR>  Bus  0, device   9, function  0:<BR>    Ethernet controller: Macronix MX98715 / MX98725 (rev 32).<BR>      Medium devsel.  Fast back-to-back capable.  IRQ 10.  Master Capable.  Late<BR>      I/O at 0x6300 [0x6301].<BR>      Non-prefetchable 32 bit memory at 0xe0001000 [0xe0001000].<P>The macronix card uses the tulip driver.<P>And the fact that any new version of a Linux distro will run on an old 386 now is obsolete.  The libc libraries are to big and memory intensive.  You are hard pressed to get it to install in even 16MB of RAM.  I got it to install in 24MB, but it would crap out with 16.||||reg||1
Z||000026||btoth||10-29-2001||09:31 PM||||SteveJ,<P>I remember in my networking class we worked with Novell 3.1 under DOS (don't laugh! the class was pathetic!) we had to run a special setup untility to change the setting in the Ethernet card's BIOS (I think someone mentioned this already).  Then, you would have to write those settings down, and in Redhat, you'd have to manualy setup the card.<P>Or you could find an old Ethernet card with manual jumpers to set the settings.||||reg||6
Z||000027||87GTZ34||10-30-2001||11:04 AM||||Cliff, You're too modest...  Calling youself an average user.  I agree with Mach10 that most home users won't be able to run linux until a very forgiving package is released.  I am a computer guy by background and have worked with UNIX extensively. I also have done some consulting for folks with their own businesses and small computer networks.  In my professional opinion none of those folks could use linux in it's current form and available apps.  However if a 'home enthusiast' has an aptitude for computers/programming they certainly could come up to speed and implement linux successfully.  It probably is no different than people with mechanical ability working fieros or cars in general.||||reg||6
Z||000028||Red2m4||10-30-2001||12:20 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B>So here's my question. Everyone whines and complains about how much Windows sucks arse. SO WHY HASN'T ANYONE COME UP WITH A REASONABLE ALTERNATIVE?!?!? C'mon people, it's not THAT hard....</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Remember OS2?  IBM's OS???  Thats ok, not many people do.  Was only about 7 or 8 years ago, it flopped.  <P>It would probably require federal government intervention to break Microsofts hold on the operating system market.  This, I think, would be bad.  If Bill Gates cant make windows run smoothly, what are the odds that George Bush can?  <P>In the future, maybe 20 -30 years, I think computer hardware  and software technology will get to the point where an operating system is kind of obsolete.  It will be more based on artificial intelligence and the like.  <P>Of course, this is far from my area of expertise.  (I know quite well that I'm talking out of my A$$)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000029||Cliff Pennock||10-30-2001||12:55 PM||||SteveJ, I'm sorry it still doesn't work for you. I can remember some 3Com cards having some issues. You don't happen to have a spare (non-3Com) NIC laying around you could try instead?<P>About your soundblaster not being recognized, it sounds like your are using an older version of the RedHat distribution. The newer versions (7+) should be able to recognize it without problems.||||reg||1
Z||000030||Mach10||10-30-2001||08:03 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cliff Pennock:<BR><B> I must disagree here. There really isn't anything I can [b]not</B> do in Linux that I can do in Windows. Except for a few games perhaps. And like I said earlier, most programs for Linux are free. On the other hand, there are <B>lots</B> of things I can do in Linux that I can't do in Windows.<P>The biggest problem is knowing where to find all these programs. Since all programs are free, you can't simply go to Best Buy to get your software. You need to know where to find it on the internet.[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Well, my meaning may have slipped here  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>first off, I have yet to see anything awe-inspiring on Linux. True, the software is free, but by that token, most of the software is custom stuff designed to solve a very specific problem. You will NOT find anything mainstream or worthwhile on Linux. New games will only be ported some time after their release. No programmer wants to do it for free... I sure as hell wouldn't  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Now, if you are a reasonably advanced user who has no difficulty learning new operating systems, or with a background in UNIX, then linux won't be too bad. Linux is very powerful, very open. There IS tons of stuff out for it. But make NO mistake, it is not for the home user, YET.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000031||Cliff Pennock||10-30-2001||08:19 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B>first off, I have yet to see anything awe-inspiring on Linux.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Man, you have seen nothing yet!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>StarOffice is an awesome set of free office programs (like MSOffice), Gimp is an awesome free paint program, Unreal Tournament is awesome on Linux, so is Tribes2 (just a few of the many Linux ports of games available). Even Netscape screams on Linux. KDE is an awesome GUI for Linux (love the themes, they're unlike Windows themes because you can change the appearance of <B>everything</B>), and the list goes on and on. But most awe-inspiring is its stability. I had some friends who were anti-Linux but are now avid Linux users after I had showed them how it ran on my system.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000032||Mach10||10-30-2001||09:13 PM||||I ain't Anti-linux... I just have no confidence in it as a platform yet. It's entire concept is anti-progressive. People will get bored of it, there's no incetive to develop. Eventually, you'll be stuck with what's out there, because no-one feels like doing anything else. I can't stand that idea... Yeah, the game-ports on the system are great... But they take a while to come out, and all the really cool games will never make it 'cos the software houses don't feel like losing the cash.<P>I've run linux, am fully aware of the potential... But <I>in potentia</I> is where it will likely remain, for the home user...<P>As for making battle-boxes and firewalls, nuthin' beats the 'nux  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I O\|/n Jo0!<P>||167.1.120.20||reg||10
Z||000033||Cliff Pennock||10-30-2001||09:31 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B>I ain't Anti-linux... I just have no confidence in it as a platform yet. It's entire concept is anti-progressive. People will get bored of it, there's no incetive to develop.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>But there is. It's being developed by hundreds of people, most of them students that do this as part of their study projects. That's why development will never stop. And it has already been around for years. And as long as there are anti-Microsoft people, there will be Linux developers.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>Yeah, the game-ports on the system are great... But they take a while to come out, and all the really cool games will never make it 'cos the software houses don't feel like losing the cash.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Tribes2 (my favorite game at this moment) was almost out day and date with the Win version. Have a look at <A HREF="http://www.lokigames.com," TARGET=_blank>www.lokigames.com,</A>  they are the ones who are porting all the cool games.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000034||Mach10||10-30-2001||09:45 PM||||Actually, I'm pissed off at both MS and the Linux community:<P>MS: No competition: No effort into the software == JUNK<BR>'Nux: that's just it. University student 'lets see if I can do this..." makes up the entire software pool... Again, it's hot now, but it will peter out. There is no quality control, other than what you make for yourself. There's no incentive to make a program that works on anything other than your own system... There's almost no driver support for popular hardware (although this is starting to change) due to the almost weekly Kernel changes... Linux is not a very good idea.  But again, what it does, it excells at. I'm just saying that it will plateau soon, and begin to drop off... If someone comes out with a commercial replacement for the Windows system, and it's any good at all, Windows will go down, and so will linux  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>hehehe... I'm arguing with cliff! AND getting away with it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  We should start trading insults soon, don'tcha think?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ||||reg||10
Z||000035||Standard||10-30-2001||09:58 PM||||I remember when I had to choose between IBM's PC-DOS and the good ole' MS-DOS.  And I'm only 20!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>My dad was a hardcore OS/2 fan back in the day.  I remember playing around on it a little bit.. ahhh, the good ole' days of the 386 20Mhz overclocked to 25Mhz.  hehehe.  My comp now is 52 times faster and has more memory than that 386 had in hard drive space.  My video card has more memory than that machine did!  Makes me want to dust off our P-120 that's sitting in the back room and install windows 95  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_pic_full.jpg">          <BR><FONT COLOR="red">1988 Quad 4 Coupe 5 speed</FONT><BR><FONT SIZE="1"><FONT COLOR="white">1986 GT 5 speed, 1984 SC Auto</FONT><BR><FONT COLOR="blue">AOL: LastMinuteBastrd  AIM: SavedbyJebus</FONT></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000036||Hippocrat||10-31-2001||12:09 AM||||IMHO<P>It's the fear of these *NIX operating systems that keeps Windoze on top.<P>It is true that these operating systems take a little more effort and knowledge to implement, but the trade off is a good one.<BR>You may have to replace some hardware such as software (Win) modems.<P>There is a catch 22 however.<BR>No one wants to develop software for these platforms because the user base is significantly lower than that of Windows.<BR>and<BR>No one wants to take the time to learn an operating system that apparently has little to offer the average user.<P>The best approach would be to dual boot both operating systems until you are familiar with the new one.<P>I personally have little to no experience with Linux I tend to favor FreeBSD. From what I've heard and read they are cut from pretty much the same cloth different but the same. <P>And if you like the traditional Windows Desktop, it is possible to install a window manager under the Xwindows system that mimics the desktop almost exactly.<P>There are windows emulators available, I believe lindows was mentioned. I've had Microsoft Office 97 pro running under WINE on a FreeBSD Box. I don't know any good Linux resources but if you want to read more about FreeBSD there is amazing user support and documentation available from their Web site.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.freebsd.org" TARGET=_blank>http://www.freebsd.org</A> <P>Just my two cents.||||reg||1
Z||000037||Hippocrat||10-31-2001||12:26 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Jefrysuko:<BR><B>I would be interested in running this FVWM 95 at home if I knew more about it.  Unfortunately Windows is just to friendly of an OS for a lazy guy like me to pass up.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I believe it was addressed before (I think)<P>FVWM 95 is one of those windows managers for the Xwindows system that runs on operating systems like Linux and FreeBSD. After you get it up and running you can just change the look of it by changing the window manager<BR>from FVWM 95 to something like black box or one of the others that btoth mentioned||||reg||1
Z||000038||Monkeyman||10-31-2001||02:02 AM||||Can anyone expand on the dual boot thing?  I understand that it's a way to have more than one OS but what does it take to do it?  Will I lose all of the pics, programs, links, etc that I have currently saved on my computer?  I want to try out Linux, but I think I should keep Windows around for a while until I'm comfortable with Linux.<P>Anyone give me some help here?  Cliff, maybe?  (I told you I don't sleep much.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> )||||reg||1
Z||000039||Hippocrat||10-31-2001||02:22 AM||||It is possible to retain your data while getting the drive ready for a second operating system.<P>If your comfortable with Partition resizers like Partition magic. It is possible to shrink your partition and create a new one. I so far (knock wood) have never lost any data using these utilities, but it can happen. Especially if you do not defrag your disk first.<P>Forgive me if this is obvious but. a partition is just a section of your hard drive. you have like Drive C: and D: (and your CD rom(s) ) showing in my computer even though you only have one hard drive. <P>It can be done. Windows should be the first operating system on the drive. Makes it easier anyway, put it that way for now.<P>There is actually a free partition resizer out there I used it a few times before I picked up partition magic. it works well Ill see if I can find a link to it. <P>Just a Note if your not comfortable with partitions, you really do want to read a lot before actually using any of the applications available. And if you have a burner definitely back up anything you cant replace easily. (Just incase).<P>After you have created a second partition on your hard drive, it is a simple matter of installing your second OS. I am not sure about Linux but you can actually install FreeBSD from a DOS (Windows) partition. Just like you were installing another Program. The next time you turn your computer on you will be asked which one you want to start Windows or your other operating system.<P>Hope some of that made sense. <p>[This message has been edited by Hippocrat (edited 10-31-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000040||87GTZ34||10-31-2001||11:37 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Hippocrat:<BR><B>IMHO<P>It's the fear of these *NIX operating systems that keeps Windoze on top.<P>It is true that these operating systems take a little more effort and knowledge to implement, but the trade off is a good one.<BR>You may have to replace some hardware such as software (Win) modems.<P>There is a catch 22 however.<BR>No one wants to develop software for these platforms because the user base is significantly lower than that of Windows.<BR>and<BR>No one wants to take the time to learn an operating system that apparently has little to offer the average user.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well this is slowy changing. As Cliff mentioned there many, many independent developers and students developing but the problem is packaging for the non-computer person.<P>Also, IBM is using Linux for their servers to help combat security and reliability issues for their customers.  I guess they don't want to put their eggs in the MS basket where there is a big liability and their reputation is at stake.||||reg||1
Z||000041||Cliff Pennock||10-31-2001||12:41 PM||||Just to show how serious Linux is considered as an alternative, take a look at Sharps newest line of PDAs: <A HREF="http://developer.sharpsec.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://developer.sharpsec.com/</A> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000042||btoth||10-31-2001||12:55 PM||||I was just at CompUSA and they had Win4Linux (or something similar).  A commercial Windows emulator that runs in a window under Linux.<P>As far as having both Win and Linux on the same HD, first you would want to completely defrag your HD.  This <I>should</I> move all Windows files the "top" (if you're looking at a defrag window).  Then there is a program called Fips that comes on the RedHat CD under the DOS Tools folder.  This will allow you to easily split your HD into two partitions.  Then, when you're installing Linux, your Windows drive will be hda0 (zero, not O) and the Linux partion will be hda1.  (Even Win2k recognizes your HDs this way.)  Make sure you <B>don't</B> install over hda0, that would wipe out Windows.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Then, (how many thens are in this??) the Linux install will ask you if you want to install Lilo, the boot loader. It will setup your computer to prompt you for either Windows or Linux when you boot your computer.  (This can be uninstalled later if you don't want it, or you can do what I do and just install it on a boot floppy and not on the master boot record.  Then when I want Linux, I just pop in the floppy - also good for recoveries.)<P>Durring your install at some point, there should be a time to select different "packages" that you want to install.  There should be some for DOS sharring or DOS connectivity or something similar.  That will allow you to map Windows(DOS) drives under Linux, so that you can access all your files.  I just map my whole C: drive to a folder call "Windows".<P>So, yes, you can still access all your Windows files.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>And that StarOffice that Cliff mentioned is supposed to be completly compatible with MS Office.  I've never had an opertunity to use it though.<p>[This message has been edited by btoth (edited 10-31-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000043||SteveJ||10-31-2001||04:35 PM||||Well last night I got the sound driver activated with a command line utility called sndconfig. I now have system prompts through the sound blaster but the cd player isn't playing through it. This is what I mean when I say not ready for prime time. <P>As far as the network card is concerned, its a big befuzelment. Red Hat tells me to go to another site where they have the driver as source code ( tulip.c ) and want me to recompile the kernel. To me that's an, "Oh, get serious" kind of thing. The only way I can read that site is with windows but then they say due to differences in file formats I must use a utility called DosToLinux to change the line returns or something so that the compiler won't reject the file. WTF<BR>Red Hat can't just keep the compiled driver on their site? - Not ready for prime time.<P>Power users have no problems? I'm not exactly a chump at this and I'm confused as to how to do it. I have five big books here and it's still mud as to what is required and what the operating system can deal with.<P>There is a very sharp learning curve here which is exacerbated by the install not doing a good enough job.||||reg||1
Z||000044||Cliff Pennock||10-31-2001||05:09 PM||||What version <B>are</B> you using??? The latest RedHat version has an installer very similar to Windows' and there was no need what so ever to recompile the kernel (PS: recompiling the kernel is nothing more than clicking a button, not much more to it than "restarting windows" after you've installed a new program).<P>The soundcard should be recognized automatically. If there's no support for your NIC, then it's either a very old NIC, or not a mainstream one.<P>PS: Also be aware that there are hundreds of thousands of people with the exact same problem you are experiencing (soundcard not recognized, NIC not recognized) and they are using <B>Windows</B>!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000045||SteveJ||10-31-2001||06:52 PM||||Red Hat 7.0 ( or the older Mandrake 7.0 )<BR>Sound Blaster AWE64<BR>LinkSys NC100 card, uses the very common DEC 21040 chip which requires the tulip.o driver ver .92.<BR>Computer is a 450 PII with the common 440Bx motherboard. 256Meg memory.<P>Maybe tomorrow I'll set the BIOS to failsafe settings and see if it goes then. Havn't tried that yet.||||reg||1
Z||000046||Cliff Pennock||10-31-2001||07:47 PM||||I use RH7.2. Uses the new 2.4.x kernel and a much better installer.||||reg||1
Z||000047||SteveJ||11-01-2001||09:02 PM||||Ah, Ha ha ha ha<BR>Ah, Ha ha ha ha<P>Red hat has a support forum for users to help each other. They must not monitor it to closely. The guys over there are joking that the boys at Red Hat must have gone to the store and bought one of each net card available and taken them back to the lab to make sure they didn't work with their OS.<P>Seems net cards requiring the tulip.o driver have "known problems" in RH7.0 which were fixed in 7.1 but returned in 7.2<P>Hey, no net card = no cable modem = no internet = no interest.||||reg||1
Z||000048||btoth||11-02-2001||09:28 AM||||What brand NIC card is that?  Why not just go buy a cheap 3-Com or Linksys.  Guaranteed to work.<P>Sndconfig should automatically find your soundcard seeing as how it's a CreativeLabs.  It even recognizes my crappy Soundblaster clone.  <P>As far as the CD player... does it spin your CD-ROM when you play it?  If not, you have to mount the CD-ROM.  Are there any setup options for the CD-Player?  It always worked just fine for me. Did you turn up the volume under X-Windows?  It has it's own volume controls just like Windows.  One for CD, sound files, mic., etc.<p>[This message has been edited by btoth (edited 11-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000049||SteveJ||11-02-2001||07:49 PM||||I'm worken on it - I'm worken on it. I'll get it. Just takes time.<P>Card is a Linksys NC100. Uses the very common DEC 21040 chip and is not recognized despite the fact it should be. From reading the troubleshooting sites it's not that uncommon a problem.||||reg||1
Z||000050||SteveJ||11-30-2001||02:55 PM||||Greetings from Linux and Mozilla. I finally got it up to speed.<BR>Most recently the Mandrake 8.1 disk I had was defective and failed during installation telling me a directory was missing. <P>But. . . the Red Hat 7.1, supplied by my son, went flawlessly. If this had been my first try I, like some, would wonder how there could be problems. It recognized the netcard and setup the internet connection. It even recognized the Sound Blaster although it doesn't play a CD through the Blaster. ||||reg||1
Z||000051||Formula88||11-30-2001||03:11 PM||||Yup, Linux has one drawback compared to Windoze.  Windoze is more user friendly.  Also, Linux expects you to be at a certain technical level.  Windoze is designed so that morons can use it.<P>Now don't think I'm dissing anyone on Windoze.  I'm running it myself right now.  It's just that the products are designed for different types of users.  With time, Linux can be made as user friendly and automatic as Windows, but it would likely loose some of it's customizeability.  <P>Linux can do anything Windows can do, but faster, and with fewer resources.  It does require the user to have more technical expertise, though.  Also, if you're having problems with Linux, remember this:  How long have you been using Windows?  Years?  How long have you been using Linux?  Hours? (assuming you're trying it for the first time) Linux is very different, so it takes a while to get used to how it works.||||reg||1
